package com.hpcai270

/** Additional depth layer for the 45 published days. These notes are intentionally practical: terminology, failure modes, commands and interview-level reasoning. */
fun depthNote(day: Int): String = when (day) {
    1 -> "DEEP DIVE — HPC IS A SYSTEM, NOT A SERVER\n\nKnow the distinction between compute, memory, interconnect, storage, scheduling and software. A high-FLOPS GPU cluster can still perform badly if the data path or synchronization path is weak. Learn to think end-to-end: application → CPU/GPU → memory → NIC/fabric → filesystem/storage → back again. In interviews, avoid defining HPC as simply ‘many computers’. The important idea is coordinated parallel work with measurable scaling.\n\nREAL QUESTION: if GPU utilization is low, investigate data loading, CPU preprocessing, memory pressure, network communication, filesystem latency and checkpointing before blaming the GPU.\n\nFIELD HABIT: always draw the data path before troubleshooting.",
    2 -> "DEEP DIVE — CLUSTER ROLES\n\nSeparate login, management/control, compute, storage and service nodes conceptually. Login nodes are usually for preparation and submission, not heavy production computation. Compute nodes are where jobs consume CPU/GPU resources. Management services maintain cluster health, scheduling and configuration. Shared storage provides common data; local NVMe often provides scratch/cache.\n\nKnow why these roles may be physically separated or combined depending on cluster size. Also understand out-of-band management, BMC/IPMI/Redfish, provisioning, DNS, NTP, LDAP/identity and monitoring at a high level.\n\nINTERVIEW: explain a job's journey from SSH login through Slurm allocation to a GPU node and shared filesystem.",
    3 -> "DEEP DIVE — CPU, GPU AND MEMORY\n\nA CPU emphasizes flexible control flow, large caches and a smaller number of powerful cores. A GPU emphasizes massive parallel arithmetic. AI performance also depends on HBM capacity/bandwidth, PCIe, NVLink/NVSwitch and host memory. Understand the difference between compute throughput and data movement.\n\nA common path is storage → NIC → host memory → GPU memory → GPU. Every hop has bandwidth and latency. Multi-GPU systems add GPU-to-GPU communication; multi-node systems add fabric communication.\n\nFIELD RULE: never say ‘GPU is faster’ without specifying the workload and precision. Explain why matrix/tensor-heavy operations map well to GPUs and why serial/control-heavy work may remain CPU-oriented.",
    4 -> "DEEP DIVE — THE HPC SOFTWARE STACK\n\nKnow what sits between hardware and applications: firmware, kernel, drivers, CUDA/ROCm, MPI, NCCL, OpenMP, libraries, modules, schedulers, filesystems and monitoring. Version compatibility matters: a working GPU driver, CUDA runtime and framework combination is part of the platform.\n\nEnvironment modules are important because clusters may provide several compiler, MPI and CUDA versions simultaneously. Containers add another packaging layer, but they still depend on kernel, device and network capabilities.\n\nTROUBLESHOOTING: identify the first failing layer. An application timeout can originate from DNS, a filesystem mount, a driver, a network path or a storage target. Do not jump directly to the storage array.",
    5 -> "DEEP DIVE — JOBS AND SCHEDULING\n\nA scheduler converts resource requests into allocations. Learn job state, partition/queue, priority, fair-share, reservations, backfill, wall time and resource constraints. With Slurm, recognize sbatch, srun, squeue, sinfo, scancel, sacct and scontrol.\n\nA pending job can be healthy: it may be waiting for GPUs, memory, a node constraint, fair-share priority or a reservation. A running job can still be slow because the requested resources are available but the application is I/O- or communication-bound.\n\nSTORAGE CONNECTION: checkpoint storms can create a burst of writes from many jobs simultaneously. Capacity planning must consider workload concurrency, not only total capacity.",
    6 -> "DEEP DIVE — PARALLELISM AND SCALING\n\nUnderstand strong scaling versus weak scaling. Strong scaling asks how runtime changes for the same problem as workers increase. Weak scaling increases problem size with worker count. Speedup is limited by serial work, synchronization, communication, load imbalance and I/O.\n\nAmdahl's law gives the intuition: a small serial fraction can dominate as worker count becomes large. Storage has the same property: adding more targets only helps if clients can generate enough parallel I/O and the filesystem can distribute it.\n\nFIELD EXERCISE: imagine 64 GPU nodes sharing one storage path. List every point where serialization could occur. That list is the beginning of performance engineering.",
    7 -> "DEEP DIVE — MPI\n\nKnow rank, communicator, point-to-point operations and collective operations. Broadcast, reduce, all-reduce, gather and all-to-all have different traffic patterns. MPI performance depends on process placement, CPU affinity, NIC selection, topology, latency, bandwidth and congestion.\n\nFor storage engineers, the key connection is that tightly coupled applications may synchronize frequently. One slow node or network path can delay the whole job. An I/O pause can also shift communication timing and create secondary congestion.\n\nPRACTICE: draw four ranks across two nodes and identify the network path for a collective. Then ask which metrics would prove whether the fabric or storage is responsible for the slowdown.",
    8 -> "DEEP DIVE — OPENMP AND NUMA\n\nOpenMP creates threads inside shared-memory processes. Learn parallel regions, work sharing and thread affinity conceptually. Large servers are often NUMA systems: each CPU socket has local memory and access to remote memory can have different latency/bandwidth.\n\nNUMA matters to storage because I/O interrupts, network queues, CPU affinity and memory buffers all interact. A benchmark can look different depending on where the process and memory are placed.\n\nFIELD RULE: when investigating performance, know CPU count, socket layout, NUMA nodes, thread count, process count and I/O concurrency. A ‘storage’ benchmark can actually be limited by CPU scheduling or memory bandwidth.",
    9 -> "DEEP DIVE — HPC STORAGE PERFORMANCE\n\nDefine the workload before measuring it. Record block size, access pattern, read/write ratio, concurrency, queue depth, client count, dataset size, filesystem, cache state and test duration. Distinguish single-client performance from aggregate cluster performance.\n\nA storage platform may have excellent peak bandwidth but poor small-file metadata performance, or excellent random IOPS but insufficient sustained bandwidth for training. Different AI phases create different pressure: dataset ingestion, training reads, checkpoint writes, validation and inference serving.\n\nINTERVIEW: explain why ‘the array can do 20 GB/s’ is not a sufficient performance statement.",
    10 -> "DEEP DIVE — PARALLEL FILESYSTEMS\n\nUnderstand the separation of metadata and data. Lustre uses metadata services/targets and object storage servers/targets. IBM Storage Scale evolved from GPFS and provides a parallel shared filesystem/data platform. BeeGFS is another important HPC filesystem.\n\nLearn the terms namespace, inode, metadata operation, data target, stripe, client, mount and recovery. The design objective is parallel access, not simply a larger directory tree.\n\nREAL-WORLD: DDN EXAScaler is a commercial Lustre platform used for HPC/AI environments. Learn the architecture independent of vendor branding; then map vendor components onto the architecture.",
    11 -> "DEEP DIVE — NVMe\n\nKnow why NVMe was created for PCIe solid-state media rather than inheriting the limitations of legacy disk-oriented protocols. Learn controller, namespace, submission queue, completion queue, queue pairs, PCIe lanes and NVMe-oF as separate concepts.\n\nUseful Linux tools include nvme list, nvme id-ctrl, nvme smart-log, lsblk, lspci and iostat. Health data can include temperature, percentage used, media errors and available spare.\n\nPERFORMANCE: queue count, queue depth, CPU placement, PCIe topology, firmware and thermal behavior can all matter. Do not treat an NVMe SSD as a magical unlimited-speed device.",
    12 -> "DEEP DIVE — NVMe-oF\n\nNVMe over Fabrics extends the NVMe command model to remote storage. Know the major transports: NVMe/TCP, NVMe/RDMA and NVMe/FC. The transport affects latency, CPU overhead, network requirements and operational tooling.\n\nTrace: application → filesystem/block layer → NVMe initiator → fabric → target → NVMe media. Failure domains include discovery, authentication, routing, MTU, link health, queueing and target availability.\n\nSTORAGE-ADMIN CONNECTION: your SAN knowledge maps well to target/initiator thinking, but NVMe-oF changes the protocol stack and performance expectations.",
    13 -> "DEEP DIVE — RDMA\n\nRDMA enables direct memory-to-memory data movement with reduced CPU involvement compared with traditional socket paths. Learn HCA/NIC, queue pair (QP), completion queue (CQ), memory registration, work request and protection domain at conceptual level.\n\nRDMA is a programming/data-movement model; InfiniBand and RoCE are fabrics/transports that can carry RDMA. This distinction matters in interviews.\n\nTROUBLESHOOTING: verify link state, device discovery, subnet/fabric configuration, PFC/ECN for RoCE where applicable, counters, MTU and application-level errors. ‘RDMA is broken’ is not a useful diagnosis without identifying the failing layer.",
    14 -> "DEEP DIVE — INFINIBAND AND ROCE\n\nInfiniBand is purpose-built for high-performance interconnects and includes RDMA semantics. RoCE carries RDMA over Ethernet and therefore introduces Ethernet operational concerns such as congestion control and loss handling.\n\nLearn HCA, leaf/spine or fat-tree topology, ports, links, routing, QoS, virtual lanes, PFC, ECN and congestion. NVIDIA Quantum switches, ConnectX adapters and UFM are common names to recognize.\n\nINTERVIEW: compare InfiniBand and RoCE without saying one is simply ‘faster’. Discuss fabric design, operational model, congestion behavior, ecosystem and workload requirements.",
    15 -> "DEEP DIVE — THE COMPLETE HPC/AI DATA PATH\n\nTraining is a loop: dataset access → preprocessing → host memory → GPU memory → compute → collective synchronization → checkpoint. Storage can be remote shared parallel storage or local NVMe cache/scratch.\n\nThe expensive resource is often the accelerator. If the data path cannot keep GPUs busy, the cluster's theoretical FLOPS are not converted into useful application throughput.\n\nCAPSTONE QUESTION: given 1,000 GPUs and poor utilization, build a troubleshooting tree covering storage throughput, metadata, network congestion, CPU preprocessing, GPU memory, NCCL communication and application batching. This is the mindset expected of AI infrastructure engineers.",
    16 -> "DEEP DIVE — LINUX FOR HPC\n\nLearn processes, services, filesystems, devices, permissions, networking and logs as one operating system model. Know systemd at a high level, kernel messages, `/proc`, `/sys`, udev and device naming.\n\nStorage engineers should be comfortable with lsblk, lsscsi, findmnt, df, du, mount, ps, top, free, vmstat, iostat, ip, ss, ethtool, dmesg and journalctl.\n\nFIELD RULE: collect evidence before changing configuration. Record timestamps, symptoms, affected hosts and whether the issue is reproducible.",
    17 -> "DEEP DIVE — LINUX FILESYSTEMS\n\nDistinguish block devices, partitions, filesystems and mounts. Understand inode, directory entry, permissions, hard link, symbolic link, filesystem metadata and mount options.\n\nKnow the difference between capacity and inode exhaustion. A filesystem can have free GB but no free inodes. Understand local filesystems such as ext4/xfs conceptually and how network/parallel filesystems present a POSIX namespace.\n\nHPC CONNECTION: the application sees a filesystem abstraction, but beneath it may be many servers, network paths and storage targets.",
    18 -> "DEEP DIVE — PROCESSES AND I/O\n\nA process has an address space, file descriptors, threads and scheduling state. Learn how to identify a process, inspect CPU usage, open files and wait states. I/O-heavy processes may spend time waiting rather than consuming CPU.\n\nUseful tools: ps, top, pidstat, lsof and `/proc/<pid>`. Understand that a process stuck in I/O wait is a symptom, not proof that the storage array is faulty.\n\nFIELD EXERCISE: run a file copy or FIO test in a Linux VM and observe the process, CPU, memory and disk statistics simultaneously.",
    19 -> "DEEP DIVE — MEMORY\n\nKnow RAM, page cache, buffers, virtual memory, swap and memory pressure. Linux may use free memory as cache; ‘used memory’ alone does not mean a problem.\n\nStorage performance can be distorted by caching. A benchmark reading the same small dataset repeatedly may measure memory/cache behavior instead of backend storage. Large AI pipelines also depend on host memory for staging and preprocessing.\n\nTools: free -h, vmstat, /proc/meminfo and sar. Learn to distinguish capacity pressure, reclaim activity and actual swapping.",
    20 -> "DEEP DIVE — PERMISSIONS AND IDENTITY\n\nKnow user/group IDs, mode bits, ownership, ACLs and the difference between local identity and directory-service identity. In shared HPC environments, consistent UID/GID mapping is critical.\n\nNFS can expose identity mismatches. Parallel filesystems add their own client/server security and namespace considerations. Containers can introduce another identity layer.\n\nTROUBLESHOOTING: separate ‘permission denied’ from ‘path unavailable’, ‘mount failed’ and ‘filesystem read-only’. Check ownership, mode, ACLs, identity mapping and export/filesystem policy before changing permissions blindly.",
    21 -> "DEEP DIVE — LINUX NETWORKING\n\nKnow interface, MAC address, IP, subnet, gateway, route, DNS, TCP/UDP, MTU and listening socket. For HPC, add VLAN/VRF concepts, multiple interfaces, bonding, high-speed NICs and RDMA devices.\n\nTools: ip addr, ip route, ping, traceroute where appropriate, ss, ethtool and tcpdump at a basic level.\n\nSTORAGE CONNECTION: an NFS mount can fail because of DNS or routing; a slow filesystem can be a network bottleneck; an RDMA workload can be affected by fabric configuration. Always identify the transport before choosing tools.",
    22 -> "DEEP DIVE — LOGS AND EVIDENCE\n\nLogs are the timeline of an infrastructure incident. Learn journalctl, dmesg, kernel messages, service logs and timestamp correlation. Storage failures can appear as I/O errors, path resets, link flaps, filesystem errors or application timeouts.\n\nCorrelate client and server clocks using NTP/chrony concepts. Compare the first observed error with the later cascade.\n\nFIELD RULE: preserve the original error. Do not summarize ‘storage issue’ when the log says a specific transport, device or filesystem event.",
    23 -> "DEEP DIVE — BASH BASICS\n\nLearn variables, quoting, command substitution, pipes, redirection, exit codes, grep, awk, sed, sort, uniq and loops. These are infrastructure tools, not just programming exercises.\n\nA safe operational script validates input, handles failures, writes useful output and avoids destructive defaults. Understand `$?`, `set -euo pipefail` conceptually and why quoting variables matters.\n\nLAB: build a script that checks disk usage on a list of test hosts and produces a simple report. Never start by automating production changes.",
    24 -> "DEEP DIVE — BASH AUTOMATION\n\nMove from one-liners to reusable scripts: functions, arguments, configuration files, logging, retries and concurrency. Learn SSH-based automation carefully and understand idempotence: running a safe check twice should not create two different states.\n\nFor HPC/storage work, automation targets include health checks, mount validation, performance collection, inventory and benchmark orchestration.\n\nINTERVIEW: explain how you would write a script that checks 100 Linux nodes and distinguishes unreachable hosts from hosts with unhealthy storage.",
    25 -> "DEEP DIVE — PYTHON FOR INFRASTRUCTURE\n\nYou do not need to become a software engineer first. Learn variables, lists/dicts, functions, modules, exceptions, file handling, JSON, subprocess and HTTP/API basics.\n\nPython is useful for parsing command output, generating reports, calling REST APIs, collecting metrics and orchestrating repetitive tasks.\n\nFIELD PROJECT: read a small JSON inventory, validate required fields, run a safe local check and write a CSV/Markdown report. Later this skill becomes useful for storage APIs, Slurm tooling and performance analysis.",
    26 -> "DEEP DIVE — BLOCK, FILE AND OBJECT\n\nBlock exposes a device-like abstraction; the host or storage stack controls the filesystem above it. File storage exposes a shared namespace and POSIX/SMB-style semantics. Object storage exposes API-addressed objects and metadata.\n\nKnow FC, iSCSI and NVMe-oF as block-oriented technologies; NFS, SMB, Lustre and Storage Scale as file-oriented examples; S3 as an object API. Products can support more than one model.\n\nARCHITECTURE QUESTION: choose the interface based on workload semantics, not brand familiarity.",
    27 -> "DEEP DIVE — NFS\n\nLearn export, mount, NFS versions, RPC, client/server roles, UID/GID, permissions, mount options and network dependencies. NFS is excellent foundational knowledge even when the production HPC filesystem is Lustre or Storage Scale.\n\nTroubleshooting order: name resolution/connectivity → route/firewall → export → mount/version → identity/permissions → server health → backend storage → workload behavior.\n\nPERFORMANCE: client count, RTT, network bandwidth, server CPU, filesystem/cache behavior and backend latency all matter.",
    28 -> "DEEP DIVE — S3\n\nKnow bucket, object, key, metadata, endpoint, GET, PUT, LIST, DELETE, multipart upload and authorization conceptually. S3 is an API model, not a POSIX filesystem.\n\nAI platforms often keep raw datasets, training artifacts and archives in object storage, then stage hot data into high-performance filesystems or local NVMe. Object size, parallel request count and metadata operations influence performance.\n\nREAL PRODUCT: Cloudian HyperStore is an enterprise S3-compatible object-storage platform. Learn the S3 model independently of one vendor.",
    29 -> "DEEP DIVE — RAID AND PROTECTION\n\nUnderstand RAID 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10, usable capacity, rebuilds, degraded mode and write penalty. Then understand that distributed platforms may add replication or erasure coding above the drive/controller layer.\n\nA rebuild is an active workload: it consumes bandwidth, CPU and I/O resources and can change application performance. A system can be ‘healthy’ from a redundancy perspective while still being slow because it is rebuilding.\n\nINTERVIEW: explain why protection and performance are coupled engineering decisions.",
    30 -> "DEEP DIVE — METRICS\n\nIOPS describes operation rate, throughput describes bytes/sec and latency describes time. Add queue depth, concurrency, CPU utilization, network utilization, cache state and tail latency.\n\nA complete benchmark statement looks like: workload, block size, read/write mix, clients, jobs, queue depth, dataset size, duration and result.\n\nPRACTICE: compare 4 KiB random reads with 1 MiB sequential reads and explain why the same storage system can show radically different numbers.",
    31 -> "DEEP DIVE — FIO\n\nLearn filename/device, rw, bs, size, runtime, time_based, numjobs, iodepth, direct and reporting fields. Start with safe test files; destructive device tests are only for dedicated lab devices.\n\nInterpret bandwidth, IOPS and latency percentiles. Watch for cache effects, filesystem overhead and CPU saturation.\n\nLAB: run the same safe workload with queue depth 1 and 32. Record results and explain what changed. The goal is not a big number; it is understanding cause and effect.",
    32 -> "DEEP DIVE — QUEUE DEPTH\n\nOutstanding I/O lets hardware and software overlap work. Low queue depth can underutilize a parallel device; excessive queue depth can increase contention and tail latency without improving throughput.\n\nPlot throughput and latency versus queue depth. Look for the knee of the curve where throughput stops scaling while latency continues rising.\n\nHPC CONNECTION: hundreds of nodes can create huge aggregate queue pressure. Parallel filesystems, network queues and storage targets must absorb concurrency without becoming unstable.",
    33 -> "DEEP DIVE — LATENCY\n\nThink in percentiles: median, p95, p99 and beyond. Tail latency matters when many workers synchronize or when a small number of slow operations delay a larger workflow.\n\nBreak end-to-end latency into client software, filesystem, network, server/service and media. Measure at multiple layers when possible.\n\nFIELD RULE: an average can hide a serious tail. When diagnosing an AI workload, ask whether the slowest workers are waiting on I/O while the rest are ready to synchronize.",
    34 -> "DEEP DIVE — THROUGHPUT\n\nAggregate throughput is often the key HPC storage metric. But it must be related to workload demand. If 1,000 GPUs need data at a certain rate, storage must deliver enough aggregate bandwidth while leaving headroom for metadata, checkpoints and other tenants.\n\nScaling can be linear, sublinear or flat. Shared switches, storage servers, filesystems, client CPUs and media can all become bottlenecks.\n\nINTERVIEW: explain why a single 100 GB/s storage target does not guarantee 100 GB/s application throughput.",
    35 -> "DEEP DIVE — HPC STORAGE ARCHITECTURE\n\nKnow compute nodes, management nodes, high-speed fabric, parallel filesystem services, metadata, data targets, local NVMe and archive/object tiers. Draw both control and data paths.\n\nReal platforms include DDN EXAScaler, IBM Storage Scale, WEKA, VAST and Dell PowerScale in different roles and architectures. Do not assume they are interchangeable products.\n\nDESIGN QUESTION: decide what belongs on local NVMe, shared parallel storage and object/archive storage for a training pipeline.",
    36 -> "DEEP DIVE — LUSTRE\n\nLearn client, MDS/MDT, OSS/OST, namespace, metadata operations, data objects and striping. Understand that opening a file is not the same operation as streaming its contents.\n\nKnow the operational vocabulary: mount, filesystem, target, stripe count, stripe size, metadata server, object storage server and recovery. Performance tuning depends heavily on workload shape.\n\nDDN EXAScaler packages Lustre into a supported enterprise platform. Learn the open architecture first, then vendor-specific management.",
    37 -> "DEEP DIVE — METADATA\n\nMetadata includes names, ownership, permissions, timestamps, directory structure and file-location information. Small-file workloads can generate huge metadata pressure even when total data volume is modest.\n\nSymptoms of metadata bottlenecks include slow creates, stats, directory operations and file opens while bulk bandwidth may look healthy.\n\nAI CONNECTION: millions of tiny training files can behave very differently from a smaller number of large shard files. Dataset design is therefore a storage-performance decision.",
    38 -> "DEEP DIVE — STRIPING\n\nStriping distributes file data across multiple storage targets. Stripe count controls how many targets can participate; stripe size controls the amount of contiguous data associated with each stripe.\n\nMore targets do not automatically mean faster performance. Small files, random access and shared contention can make aggressive striping counterproductive.\n\nLAB: draw a 4 GB file across four targets and compare it conceptually with a file stored on one target. Explain when parallel reads can benefit.",
    39 -> "DEEP DIVE — IBM STORAGE SCALE / GPFS\n\nStorage Scale evolved from GPFS and is designed for scale-out shared data access. Learn the concepts of nodes, filesets, storage pools, policies, replication/erasure coding, NSD servers and client access at a high level.\n\nUnderstand that IBM Storage Scale can span file, object and analytics-oriented workflows and is used in HPC/AI architectures.\n\nINTERVIEW: compare Storage Scale and Lustre by architecture and operational model rather than by saying one is ‘better’.",
    40 -> "DEEP DIVE — CEPH\n\nCeph is a distributed software-defined storage platform. Know MON, MGR, OSD, pool, PG, CRUSH, replication, erasure coding, RBD, CephFS and RGW/S3.\n\nThe key mental model is distributed placement plus recovery. Data movement during failure/rebalance consumes resources and can affect workloads.\n\nLAB: build a small VM Ceph cluster if hardware allows. Intentionally inspect OSD status, pool configuration and recovery behavior. Learn why Ceph is powerful but operationally different from a specialized parallel filesystem.",
    41 -> "DEEP DIVE — FABRIC TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nFor InfiniBand, learn link state, ports, HCA status, subnet management and topology tools. For RoCE, add Ethernet congestion, PFC/ECN, MTU and switch configuration.\n\nUseful NVIDIA/Mellanox-oriented commands to recognize include ibstat, ibv_devinfo, iblinkinfo, ibtracert, ibdiagnet, ethtool and mlxlink, depending on the environment.\n\nTroubleshooting rule: identify whether the failure is host NIC, cable/transceiver, switch port, routing/QoS, congestion or application-level communication.",
    42 -> "DEEP DIVE — AI STORAGE PIPELINE\n\nSeparate data preparation, training, checkpointing, validation and inference. Object storage may hold the system of record; parallel filesystem storage may serve hot training data; local NVMe may cache or stage; archive may hold long-term results.\n\nMeasure time spent in data loading and preprocessing, not just GPU kernel time. Dataset sharding, caching, prefetching, file format and concurrency can materially change GPU utilization.\n\nDESIGN EXERCISE: draw a 1 PB dataset feeding 1,000 GPUs and identify where bandwidth, metadata and checkpoint traffic occur.",
    43 -> "DEEP DIVE — NVMe PERFORMANCE\n\nGo beyond ‘NVMe is fast’. Learn PCIe generation/lane width, controller queues, namespace layout, firmware, thermal throttling, power states, queue depth and CPU/NUMA affinity.\n\nUse nvme-cli and Linux telemetry to inspect health and behavior. Compare direct device, filesystem and networked NVMe tests; they measure different layers.\n\nINTERVIEW: explain why a Gen5 NVMe SSD can still deliver poor application performance if the PCIe topology, CPU affinity, filesystem or workload is wrong.",
    44 -> "DEEP DIVE — GPU DIRECT STORAGE\n\nThe goal of GPU Direct Storage is to reduce unnecessary data copies and move data efficiently between storage/network and GPU memory where supported. The surrounding stack still matters: filesystem, NVMe/NVMe-oF, NIC, GPU driver, CUDA and application libraries.\n\nDo not treat GDS as a magic switch. Measure end-to-end application behavior and GPU utilization before and after.\n\nFIELD QUESTION: when would local NVMe, shared Lustre and GDS each make sense in an AI training design?",
    45 -> "DEEP DIVE — CAPSTONE\n\nYou should now be able to draw and explain an AI/HPC platform: GPU compute nodes, CPU/RAM, local NVMe, ConnectX/NICs, InfiniBand or RoCE fabric, scheduler, parallel filesystem, metadata services, data targets, object/archive tier and monitoring.\n\nFor troubleshooting, move from symptom → metric → layer → evidence → hypothesis → controlled test. For architecture, start from workload demand and work backward into bandwidth, latency, concurrency, capacity and resilience.\n\nFINAL INTERVIEW: design storage for a 1,000-GPU training cluster and defend every major choice.",
    else -> "DEEP DIVE — REVIEW\n\nConnect today's topic to Linux, networking, storage and AI workload behavior. Write down the vocabulary, one architecture diagram, three commands or metrics and one failure mode you can now troubleshoot."
}
